  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 PM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nCurrently, a shortwave is moving through the area overhead with an \nupstream jet approaching from the west and inverted surface \ntroughing. This has put the region in the left-exit region of the \njet, leading to large-scale lift. When combined with PWAT values of \n1.3 to 1.4 inches, this has and will continue to provide support for \nshowers and storms during the afternoon and evening. Morning \nconvection and clouds has limited heating and destabilization in \nsome areas, but up to 1,000 J/kg of MLCAPE is expected. With \nfreezing levels of 14,000 feet or higher and recent rain for some, \nefficient warm rain processes continue to emphasize localized flash \nflooding as the main concern. Some gusty winds are possible but not \nto the same degree as flooding. CAMs differ on how much coverage \nlasts overnight, but intensity will be lessened as instability is \nlost. By Thursday, troughing will have moved off to our east, \nleading to less upper support. Still, inverted surface troughing and \nlingering moisture will keep at least low-end chances for rain in \nthe area. During this time, a low in the Atlantic will be deepening \nas upper troughing moves overhead. \n\nBy Friday, the low in the Atlantic will deepen into a Nor'easter as \nit moves along the New England coast. This will lead to broad \nnorthwesterly flow across the region, pulling down cooler and drier \nair associated with high pressure to the north. Dry and more fall-\nlike weather can be expected with widespread highs in the 70s \nexpected through the weekend. Radiational cooling, especially Friday \nnight, will allow lows to drop into the 50s and upper 40s in the \nnortheast. Another warming trend is expected early next week as \nheights rise and another surface low develops in the Great Plains, \nleading to a southerly shift in winds.",
